WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:01.971
- [Reporter] The house of
representatives is gearing

00:00:01.971 --> 00:00:05.670
up to elect its new leader,
the speaker of the house.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.430
Democrats lost the majority
in the midterm elections

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:10.920
meaning Republicans will
get to name the next speaker

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.050
replacing Nancy Pelosi

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.140
- Who House Republicans
choose to be their speaker

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.594
will be the person who really sets how

00:00:18.594 --> 00:00:21.150
House Republicans work with the Senate

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.040
how they're going to
work with the White House

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.890
and House Democrats in the next two years.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.080
- [Reporter] But after a
weaker than expected midterms

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.600
performance Republicans
don't have that large

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.130
of a majority in the house

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:35.280
which could complicate the
process of choosing a speaker.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.470
So here's a look at how
the vote is shaping up

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.423
and what's at stake for both parties.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.890
Before the midterms, California Republican

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:47.070
Kevin McCarthy was largely
seen as the clear front runner.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.920
- [Natalie] Kevin McCarthy
has led House Republicans

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.560
as the minority leader
for the past four years

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:53.393
and he's done it

00:00:53.393 --> 00:00:56.010
with a singular focus on
winning the majority back.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.060
- Number one thing I'm gonna do

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.070
as a leader is I'm gonna
listen to all voices

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:02.520
in the conference and we're
gonna do together what is best.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.350
- [Reporter] But after
Republicans fail to deliver

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:05.940
a red wave in the midterms

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.880
McCarthy doesn't have the
backing that he was expecting.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.240
- The criticism has come from a handful

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.050
of Republicans who have
kind of long held beefs

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.120
with this party leader

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.260
- [Reporter] And one of those Republicans

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.140
with the beef is Arizona
Republican Andy Biggs.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.500
He's also running for speaker
while McCarthy was chosen

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.630
by the GOP to be their speaker candidate

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.940
in an internal vote in
November, the results

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.730
of that vote aren't enough
to win him the gavel.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:33.573
Here's why.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.620
On January 3rd, House
Republicans are expected to

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.760
have a majority of 222 to 212 Democrats.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.820
With one absence to be
elected, a candidate must win

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.700
at least 218 votes or the majority

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.520
of votes from those present
and voting in the roll call.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.440
But here's where it gets tricky.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:53.940
Members don't have to vote

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.890
for their party's nominated candidate.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.450
They could vote for anyone

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.970
even if they aren't serving in the house.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.520
Democrats aren't expected
to vote for McCarthy

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.310
and at least five Republicans
have voiced their opposition

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.110
to McCarthy in recent days.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.990
- I think it's far too risky
to pick Kevin McCarthy.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:12.690
I think it's risky to have
someone leading our effort going

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:16.680
into 2024 who vastly
underperformed in 2022.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.590
- So the way the math works
is Kevin McCarthy cannot

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.390
lose five votes.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:25.050
If he has four detractors,
that will get him to 218

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.931
but he's got to work to
win some of the other

00:02:27.931 --> 00:02:30.926
folks over and make sure
that the folks who haven't

00:02:30.926 --> 00:02:34.143
announced how they're voting
are going to be in his camp.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:35.963
- [Reporter] The opposition

00:02:35.963 --> 00:02:38.550
from House Republicans
varies making it difficult

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.770
for McCarthy to address every issue.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.540
- Andy Biggs, he's been critical

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.070
of Kevin McCarthy's leadership.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:47.100
Matt Gates has had long time conflicts

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.260
with Kevin McCarthy, Ralph Norman.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.610
He's kind of gone back and forth

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.730
on how adamant he is in the
opposition to Kevin McCarthy.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.500
- [Reporter] Some Republicans
have said they would

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.750
support McCarthy if he commits to changing

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.250
some conference rules

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.320
including the motion to vacate a chair

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.500
which would make it easier for
him to be ousted as speaker.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.560
McCarthy hasn't yet agreed
to back that motion.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.620
- You have a broad swath
of the House Republican

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.350
Conference that is backing
Kevin McCarthy, but

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:17.790
because their majority is
so narrow, any handful of

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:21.300
House Republicans can make
Kevin McCarthy's life difficult.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.150
- [Reporter] If McCarthy can't
garner the 218 votes needed

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.910
to become speaker, a
few things could happen.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.640
The Clerk of the House
representative Cheryl Johnson

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.580
could repeat the vote until
someone gets a majority

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.590
but that hasn't happened that often.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:35.910
The last time the house voted

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:39.630
on multiple ballots for a
speaker vote was in 1923.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:41.550
- Most Republican
lawmakers will acknowledge

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.740
that they think this will
go to multiple ballots.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.420
- [Reporter] If that doesn't
work, they could adjourn

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.060
the house essentially
calling a timeout in order

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.910
to find a solution whether
that's a different candidate

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.210
or twisting the arm of
detractors, in the year ahead.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.910
Republicans are facing a vote
on lifting the debt ceiling

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.650
and funding the federal government.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.139
Two historically tough issues

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:03.510
for Republicans who
oppose increased spending.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:04.800
- Whoever takes this job

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.210
if it's Kevin McCarthy or someone else

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:09.825
is going to be setting up
for an even tougher wrangling

00:04:09.825 --> 00:04:13.170
of House Republicans in the new Congress.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:14.793
This is only the first vote.

